    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"The aeromagnetic and aeroradioactivity maps of the Front Range region, north-central Colorado, were compiled from data acquired in 1979 during the National Uranium Resource Evaluation (NURE) program of the U.S. Department of Energy. Data for the NURE program normally were collected along 3-mile and 6-mile spaced flight lines, but in a few selected areas, such as the mountainous parts of the region discussed in this report, data were collected along flight lines spaced only one mile apart using helicopters. East of the Front Range, over the High Plains, data were collected at a flight line spacing of three miles. All flights were at 400 feet above terrain. The aeromagnetic and aeroradioactivity data for the Front Range region comprise one of the largest coherent blocks of data of these types collected concurrently in the western U.S. The maps provide synoptic overviews of the Front Range, parts of the Wet and Sangre de Cristo Mountains, and adjacent parts of Middle and South Parks, and the High Plains as indicated in the figure on the left.  The flightline data were processed to produce an internally consistent data set and were used to produce gridded data with a grid interval of 0.635 mi.  The original flightline data are available from the U.S. Geological Survey.  The references for both the original data and the data as modified by the USGS are given below.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
